        <div id="three-jobs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is the Difference Between Killing, Cleaning and Preventing Mold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Killing stops the organism, cleaning removes the stain it leaves, and preventing makes the surface harder to recolonize, and each takes a different kind of product. Knowing which of the three you are doing is the quickest way to stop buying the wrong bottle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Killing takes a registered fungicide and time</h3>

          <p>
            To kill mold, a product with fungicidal activity has to stay on the growth long enough to stop it. In the U.S., only products carrying an EPA registration number may claim to kill, and their directions give a contact time, typically several minutes with the surface kept visibly wet. The actives you will see most often in registered products are quaternary ammonium compounds, chlorine bleach, hydrogen peroxide and thymol.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cleaning is about the stain</h3>

          <p>
            Dead mold keeps its pigment. On porous grout, wood and painted concrete, that pigment lingers as gray or black blotches long after the organism has died. Bleach removes those marks better than anything else, which explains why so many bathroom cleanup sprays are built on it. The catch is that a stain remover used alone on porous material can whiten the surface while growth deeper in the material lives on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Preventing means a less welcoming surface</h3>

          <p>
            Prevention products do their work after the cleanup. Inhibitors such as Concrobium dry into a residue that discourages new growth, and a few disinfectants claim residual activity. Still, no coating outperforms moisture control. A treated surface that stays damp will grow mold again, while an untreated surface that stays dry usually will not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Most jobs need two steps</h3>

          <p>
            On basement framing or painted drywall, kill first with a registered fungicide that contains no bleach, then follow with an inhibitor. In a shower, one bleach-based remover usually handles both the kill and the stain. On siding and decks, a slow outdoor treatment spares you the scrubbing. The table pairs each common job with the pick that suits it.
          </p>

